    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что с телефонами?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What's with your phones?' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'what' straight to 'telephone'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не могу дозвониться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I can't get through.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'to get through', which directs the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Мой телефон украли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My phone was stolen.' Notice how 'my' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'stole'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мне звонила мама.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Mom called me.' Nastya links 'to me' directly with 'called' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Папа познакомился с Костей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Dad met Kostya.' Notice how 'Dad' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'Kostya'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Но он думает, что это ты.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But he thinks it's you.' Nastya uses the casual singular 'you' with 'he', as close colleagues and friends do. Then Max replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't get it.' Max links 'I' directly with 'not' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Он думает, что ты — Костя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He thinks that you are Kostya.' Notice how 'he' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'Kostya'. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Зачем он приходил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why did he come?' The question opens with the interrogative word for 'what for' and builds toward 'used to come'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Он угрожал мне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He threatened me.' Kostya combines 'he' with 'to me' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Скажи ему правду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Tell him the truth.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'say' with 'truth'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Я не могу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I can't.' The little negative word sits right in front of the verb for 'can', just like 'not' in English.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Он очень жестокий.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is very cruel.' Notice how 'he' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'cruel'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что же мне делать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What should I do?' Max links 'what' directly with 'but' to express the thought clearly. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
